    "lawCode": "Amd §367-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5872\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to the specialty\npharmacy program\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nThis bill would exempt any pharmacy owned and operated by a\nnot-for-profit organization solely for the purpose of providing drugs\nto individuals diagnosed with cystic fibrosis as part of a\ncomprehensive clinical care program approved by the national\norganization that accredits cystic fibrosis (CF) care centers provide\nsuch not for profit organization maintains a national registry of\ncystic fibrosis clinical data shall be exempt from the specialty\npharmacy program.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 amends the social services law to exempt pharmacies owned\nand operated by a not-for profit organization solely for the purpose\nof providing drugs to individuals diagnosed with cystic fibrosis as\npart of a comprehensive clinical care program approved by the national\norganization that accredits cystic fibrosis care centers and maintains\na national registry of cystic fibrosis clinical care from the\nspecialty pharmacy program.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nCystic fibrosis is an inherited chronic disease that affects the lungs\nand digestive system of about 30,000 children and adults in the United\nState (70,000 worldwide). A defective gene and its protein product\ncause the body to produce unusually thick, sticky mucus that clogs the\nlungs and leads to life-threatening lung infections; and obstructs the\npancreas and stops natural enzymes from helping the body break down\nand absorb food. In the 1950s, few children with cystic fibrosis lived\nto attend elementary school.  Today, advances in research and medical\ntreatments have further enhanced and extended life for children and\nadults with CF. The predicted age of a survival for a person with\nCystic Fibrosis is now more than 37 years of age.  Many children and\nyoung adults with CF take over twenty medications per day including\nfour orally inhaled medications with treatment times exceeding three\nhours per day.\n\nThe Cystic Fibrosis Foundation has since its inception in the 1950s\nprovided pharmacy services to patients as part of a comprehensive\nclinical support program which includes CF care centers across the\nstate.\n\nCF Services is the largest provider of pharmacy services, including\nspecialty medications, for the state's CF Medicaid patients, serving\n40% of those with the disease. Under the current criteria for the\nSpecial Pharmacy Program, CF Services is ineligible to participate as\nit does not and cannot provide the number and range or drugs required.\nThe CF pharmacy only provides drugs exclusively for the treatment of\nCystic Fibrosis.\n\nIn fact, if enacted the specialty pharmacy program as recently\nproposed would exclude the CF pharmacy which threatens the continuity\nand coordination of care. People with CF are routinely more on and off\nthe Medicaid rolls as their income fluctuates. The CF pharmacy\nprovides a safety net for those who lose their Medicaid eligibility so\nthey do not also lose access to their critical medications. CF\nServices provides emergency supplies of medications and works with\npatients to find other coverage. This allows New York Medicaid\npatients who lose eligibility to continue treatment and keeps them out\nof the hospital. This practice ultimately saves the state money since\n\nthese same patients are likely to become Medicaid eligible again and\nwill require less care if they are able to maintain their health.\n\nThe importance of the CF pharmacy to patients and the coordinated care\nof CF patients cannot be understated. Finally, and importantly, the\npharmacy continues to provide an effective fulfillment function for\nthe development of new drugs to effectively treat the disease.\n\n BILL HISTORY :\nNone.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
